1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:01,632
Same crash, same film.

2
00:00:01,652 --> 00:00:07,340
Change one word in a question, and people remember broken glass that was never there.

3
00:00:07,702 --> 00:00:15,942
In 1974, Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer showed students car-crash films and asked how fast the cars were going.

4
00:00:16,402 --> 00:00:17,859
Only the verb changed.

5
00:00:17,879 --> 00:00:22,453
'Smashed' got an average of about 41 miles per hour.

6
00:00:22,473 --> 00:00:23,442
'Hit' got 34.

7
00:00:23,827 --> 00:00:28,199
Some crashes were staged at known speeds, yet the guesses barely followed.

8
00:00:28,219 --> 00:00:30,057
The word mattered more.

9
00:00:30,369 --> 00:00:36,819
In a second test, a week after the same question, students were asked: did you see any broken glass?

10
00:00:37,170 --> 00:00:38,446
There was none.

11
00:00:38,466 --> 00:00:41,197
Yet 16 of 50 in the 'smashed' group said yes.

12
00:00:41,217 --> 00:00:43,230
In the 'hit' group, 7.

13
00:00:43,624 --> 00:00:49,374
The researchers' explanation: what you saw and what you were told later blend into one memory.

14
00:00:49,829 --> 00:00:51,032
Memory isn't a recording.

15
00:00:51,052 --> 00:00:54,119
Have you ever remembered something that turned out wrong?

